      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town became a market hub in the mid‑18th century thanks to river trade and tobacco plantations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://townofprincessanne.org/about-our-town/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Eastern Shore Railroad’s southward extension boosted Princess Anne’s trade and connected it to the wider region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Princess_Anne%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Teackle Mansion, built 1802‑1819 by Littleton Dennis Teackle, now houses the Somerset County Historical Society and welcomes tours.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitsomerset.com/area-info/princess-anne",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The University of Maryland Eastern Shore, a historically black college founded in 1886, draws freshmen every fall into town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
